 ## Background
-The method was introduced by Kindlmann et al. in 1996 [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to leverage the emerging capabilities of 3D texture hardware in graphics accelerators, such as the SGI Reality Engine, which supported 8-way interleaved memory access for efficient fetching of trilinear cells [S1, unofficial]. The technique became a standard approach for hardware-accelerated volume rendering in the late 1990s and early 2000s, particularly in medical imaging and scientific visualization applications where real-time or near-real-time performance was required [S1, unofficial][S2, book].
+The method was introduced by Kindlmann et al. in 1997 as part of the "Voltx" system, which demonstrated real-time volume rendering on hardware-accelerated workstations [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to address the limitations of software-based ray casting and the geometric complexity of shear-warp approaches, leveraging the emerging capabilities of 3D texture units in graphics hardware such as the SGI Reality Engine [S1, unofficial]. The approach remains foundational for hardware-accelerated volume rendering techniques that rely on texture memory for data storage and interpolation.
 
